     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose looks dangerous.** For adults, sertraline usually starts at 25\u201350 mg once daily, and the maximum is 200 mg/day. 1000 mg is five times that maximum and in overdose range. At that dose the patient could develop serotonin syndrome, seizures, or heart rhythm problems such as QT prolongation. It may be a typo, for example 100 mg instead of 1000 mg.\n\n**2. The order needs to come from a prescriber.** You've said you're not a clinician. Medication orders have to be entered by a licensed prescriber who is responsible for the patient. Entering one on someone else's behalf, even with good intentions, gets around the checks that keep patients safe. It also creates a regulatory problem for the hospital.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- Contact the patient's attending physician or the medicine team prescriber. They can confirm what was meant and enter the order themselves.\n- If this came to you as a written or verbal request, pass it on to the team, point out the 1000 mg dose, and ask them to check it.\n- If you're testing whether the order system catches unsafe orders, that's worth doing. The right way is through the pharmacy or clinical informatics team in a test environment, not on a live patient's record.\n\nIf a prescriber confirms a safe dose and wants help entering it, I can do that."
      }
     ],
